# Rust build artifacts
target/
engine/target/
**/target/

# Tauri sidecar binary (qu.exe, built from engine/ and copied here with its
# target-triple suffix so `tauri-build`'s externalBin step can find and
# bundle it) — a build artifact, not source. Regenerate with:
#   cargo build --release --manifest-path engine/Cargo.toml -p qu-cli
# then copy engine/target/release/qu.exe to
# qu-studio-tauri/src-tauri/binaries/qu-<target-triple>.exe
qu-studio-tauri/src-tauri/binaries/

# Editor/tool working directories
.zcode/
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/

# QuStudio's per-file save-snapshot history (main.rs's `save_file` writes
# here before every overwrite) -- edit history, not project content, and
# lives beside whatever file it belongs to wherever that file happens to be.
.qu-versions/

# Node/JS build artifacts (qu-studio-tauri/, qu-ui-components/) — installed
# via npm, fully reproducible from package.json/package-lock.json, and huge
# (tens of thousands of files) — never source.
node_modules/
dist/
dist-ssr/
.vite/

# Zipped project backups (git itself is the backup mechanism going forward)
backups/*.zip

# Retired scaffolding already slated for deletion; contains a stale nested
# .git repo that would otherwise block `git add` at the parent level
_to_delete/

# A separate, unrelated Python project (~2.3GB, includes a full bundled
# Octave install + lots of scratch/.tmp files) that happens to live inside
# this directory tree — not Qu source. Remove this line if you want it
# tracked here too.
matty/

# OS cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Generated benchmark datasets — fully reproducible from the bench script's
# own seeded RNG, regenerated on every run; not source.
benchmarks/csv_filters/signal.csv
benchmarks/full_pipeline/pipeline_signal.csv
benchmarks/full_pipeline/pipeline_features.csv
benchmarks/data_wrangling/dataset.csv
benchmarks/image_processing/test_image.bmp
benchmarks/image_processing/*_output_*.bmp
benchmarks/file_io/*.bin
benchmarks/file_io/*.csv
benchmarks/file_io/*.bmp
benchmarks/file_io/*.png
benchmarks/large_pipeline/pipeline_signal*.csv
benchmarks/large_pipeline/pipeline_features*.csv
# advanced_validation/peak_finding's 3 noise-level signals are 200k rows
# each (~4MB) -- large enough to skip committing like the above, unlike
# the small (<40KB) close_peaks/ambiguous_pair/plateau CSVs in the same
# dir, which stay committed (shallow_ml/mlp convention: small enough that
# byte-identical cross-language reuse is worth the repo weight).
benchmarks/advanced_validation/peak_finding/low_noise.csv
benchmarks/advanced_validation/peak_finding/medium_noise.csv
benchmarks/advanced_validation/peak_finding/high_noise.csv
# image_stress's test image, same reasoning as image_processing/test_image.bmp
# above: large-ish binary, deterministic from generate_image.py's seeded RNG,
# nothing lost by not committing it. ground_truth.csv/qu_blob_stats.csv stay
# committed (small, and the whole point of the exercise is a fixed reference
# to check against).
benchmarks/advanced_validation/image_stress/test_image.bmp
benchmarks/advanced_validation/image_stress/cv2_output_binary.bmp
# advanced_validation/linalg_stress's flattened n=300/n=1000 matrix CSVs are
# 1.8MB/19.9MB (n^2 values each, one per line) -- large enough to skip
# committing like peak_finding's noise signals above; the small b_n*.csv
# (right-hand-side vectors) and x_qu_n*.csv (Qu's dumped solve results)
# stay committed, same size-based convention as the rest of this file.
# Regenerate with: python benchmarks/advanced_validation/linalg_stress/make_data.py
benchmarks/advanced_validation/linalg_stress/a_n300.csv
benchmarks/advanced_validation/linalg_stress/a_n1000.csv
# advanced_validation/table_stress's shared 30,000-row dataset.csv is
# ~2.4MB -- large enough to skip committing like the above, unlike the
# small (<1MB) group_agg_qu.csv/corrcoef_qu.csv/filter_sort_qu.csv result
# dumps in the same dir, which stay committed (deterministic, regenerated
# by table_stress.qu on every run anyway, and small enough that keeping
# them around for inspection is worth the repo weight).
benchmarks/advanced_validation/table_stress/dataset.csv
vite.config.ts.timestamp-*.mjs
.scratch-figs/
# Generated paper figures: regenerable by re-running the .qu script, and
# a filled contour map is megabytes of SVG that changes on every run.
papers/*/img/

# The assembled Overleaf tree: the .tex, .bib and biography photos are
# copied in from the original project, and everything else is produced by
# papers/why-cf-matters/build.sh from the Qu scripts.
papers/*/overleaf/img/
papers/*/overleaf/*.pdf
# This paper reuses the manuscript's own \graphicspath{{figs/}} instead of
# img/; only the four Qu-generated figures are build output, the other
# four (fig_benchmark/fig_identification/fig_lti/fig_realdata) are the
# ORIGINAL pdfs kept as fallback source material and stay tracked.
papers/*/overleaf/figs/fig_illposed.pdf
papers/*/overleaf/figs/fig_lambda.pdf
papers/*/overleaf/figs/fig_example.pdf
papers/*/overleaf/figs/fig_drift.pdf
papers/*/overleaf/*.aux
papers/*/overleaf/*.bbl
papers/*/overleaf/*.blg
papers/*/overleaf/*.log
papers/*/overleaf/*.out
papers/*/overleaf/*.fls
papers/*/overleaf/*.fdb_latexmk
papers/*/overleaf/*.png
.figwork/

# Documentation example extracts and their captured output (tools/build_docs_site.sh)
.doc-run/
.demo-run/

# Output that documentation examples write into whatever directory they
# are run from. The examples name bare relative paths on purpose -- a
# tutorial that says `savefig("waves.svg")` is teaching the reader the
# call, not a directory layout -- so running the docs drops these in the
# repository root. Anchored with a leading `/` so an actual `data.csv` or
# `config.txt` somewhere in the tree is still tracked.
/.arity-check/
/[0-9][0-9]_*.svg
/config.txt
/data.csv
/data2.csv
/results.csv
/waves.svg
/quickstart_plot.svg
/first.pdf
/first-figure.pdf
/my-first-figure.pdf

# `tools/build_function_pages.sh` builds the 700+ pages here and then
# publishes by renaming the directory into place. It is scaffolding for
# one run, 3.9 MB of it, and it is never the source of anything.
/website/.fn.new/
.link-check

# A scratch directory for comparing figure output side by side -- SVGs
# rendered two ways plus a `view.html` to flip between them. Untracked and
# unignored, it was 190 KB that any `git add website/` would sweep into an
# unrelated commit, which has happened here more than once. Ignored rather
# than deleted: it is somebody's live workspace, it just is not history.
/website/.probe/

# Qu-8 lane: rapid literature-to-Qu beta-test loop (search -> ref/ downloads
# -> synthetic test -> Qu port -> Matlab/Python port -> report). ref/ holds
# downloaded papers/reference material (copyright, not ours to publish; see
# qu-nothing-leaves-the-repo) and the whole tree churns too fast per-loop to
# be worth commit history -- Ahmed's own instruction: this directory does
# not sync to git. Promote an individual finished port out to the tracked
# private/ (see private/README.md) by hand if it earns a place there.
private/Qu8/

# Qu-8 research lane: local-only per Ahmed 2026-09-16. Never tracked,
# never synced. The working copy lives in the lane's own worktree and the
# backup on H:. `private/` itself IS tracked, so without this line an
# untracked Qu8 tree here is one broad `git add` away from four mirrors.
private/Qu8/

# Secret-pattern defensive exclusions, added 2026-09-18 after the
# ".gitignore has no explicit secret-pattern entries" hardening gap
# flagged earlier tonight was left open too long -- became actively
# relevant once the LLM provider integration started handling real
# API keys. A clean secret-history audit today doesn't prevent one
# landing tomorrow; these patterns are defense in depth, not a
# replacement for never committing a real key in the first place.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*_key
*_secret
*_token
credentials.json
credentials.*.json
secrets.json
*.p12
*.pfx
